{"Reasoning":"We need estimate the expected age at death for an American male who has already lived at least 35 years, and the probability that our estimate is within 20 years.

A reasonable approximation is to use conditional life expectancy. For a U.S. male who has survived to age 35, remaining life expectancy is roughly in the mid-40s, implying an average age at death around 80. Historical and current U.S. male life tables put life expectancy at 35 at about 44 to 47 additional years, depending on cohort and period, so an estimate near 79 to 82 is sensible.

For confidence within 20 years, the interval 60 to 100 is wide. Most adult male deaths do occur within that band, though there is some tail risk above 100 and some mortality before 60. A rough probability that the true age at death is within ±20 years of an estimate around 80 is around 0.9.","Answer":"80","Confidence":"0.9"}

{"Reasoning":"We need estimate the eventual age at death for an American female who has already lived at least 95 years. Conditional on surviving to 95, remaining life expectancy is still several years, but not huge. For U.S. women at age 95, a reasonable central estimate is around 97 to 99 years old at death, with substantial uncertainty because some will die soon after 95 and some survive to 100+.

A simple midpoint estimate is about 98 years old.

For being within 19 years of the true value, the interval would be roughly 79 to 117 years old, which is very wide and covers essentially all plausible outcomes for someone already age 95. So confidence is very high, close to 1.","Answer":"98","Confidence":"0.99"}
